# LordOS inside a Machines virtual machine (docs/VIRTUALIZATION.md 6.17, 7.2): the person signed in at the guest's seat
# may open the guest's end of org.lordos.agent, the virtio-serial port Machines adds only while the machine's guest
# agent is on. lordos-guest (lordos-guest.service, a user unit) holds it as that person. The same rule Machines' Install
# Agent writes into other Linux guests. On a computer that isn't such a guest the port doesn't exist: nothing matches.
# LordOS's own sessions (lord-sessiond) aren't logind seats, so uaccess gives nobody the port there. The port is the
# guest's administrators' (the sudo group, as Settings → Users and firstrun make them): whoever holds it gets the host's
# shell (as their own login shell), its clipboard and the files dropped on the machine, and it opens only once, so a
# group every desktop account is in (video, 0.8.9.6 in development) let a standard account take it first and read what
# the host's person typed (security findings F9, F13). An administrator can already do anything in the guest, so the
# port gives them nothing new; a standard account can't open it. Migration 013-0.8.9.6-guest-agent-admins.sh applies it.
SUBSYSTEM=="virtio-ports", ATTR{name}=="org.lordos.agent", TAG+="uaccess", GROUP="sudo", MODE="0660"
